MAILbus 400 SMTP Gateway V2.0 for Digital UNIX

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Brings together the world's global messaging systems --the Internet and
   X.400

o  Enables the seamless transfer of text, complex documents, and binary
   files between the Internet and X.400 environments through Multipurpose
   Internet Mail Extensions (MIME)

o  Supports the latest Internet and X.400 standards, plus 8 bit sendmail
   systems

o  Integrates fully with MAILbus 400 Message Transfer Agent (MTA) and DEC
   X.500 directory based backbone

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

MAILbus 400 SMTP Gateway V2.0 for Digital UNIX software is a high
performance, standards based gateway that enables users to easily exchange
simple text messages, complex documents, and binary information between the
Internet and X.400 environments, using the latest Internet and X.400
standards. Conformance to these standards, automatic operation, and full
integration enable users to send and receive messages without being
concerned about where the recipients are located in the combined network.

The high level of integration of this gateway with the Digital backbone
allows the management, performance monitoring, handling of message
downgrades, and address translation to be operated centrally. This minimizes
the amount of time spent by the system manager in managing the gateway.

MAILbus 400 SMTP Gateway V2.0 operates on the Digital UNIX platform. This
gateway continues to provide the high level of integration with the MAILbus
400 MTA (Message Transfer Agent) and DEC X.500 Directory Service based
backbone for address translation and management.

Internet standards

MAILbus 400 SMTP Gateway V2.0 conforms to a number of Internet standards
established by the Internet Engineering Task Force (IETF). These standards,
known as Request for Comment (RFC), provide the ground rules that allow the
easy transfer of information between a gateway and X.400 frameworks. These
RFCs are:

o  General messaging

   - RFC 821 -- Standard for Simple Mail Transfer Protocol (SMTP).
   - RFC 822 -- Standard for the Format of Internet Text Messages.

o  Headers and envelopes

   - RFC 1327 -- Message Header Mapping between X.400 (1988) and RFC 822.
   - RFC 1137 -- Message Header Mapping between full RFC 822 and RFC 822
     with restricted encoding; for example, specifies the handling of space,
     underscore, and brackets in addresses.
   - RFC 1522 -- MIME Part 2 (to be supported in a later release of MAILbus
     400 SMTP Gateway).

o Bodyparts

   - RFC 1521 -- MIME Part 1 (Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions).
     Mechanisms for specifying and describing the format of Internet message
     bodies. Defines the actual message bodypart and allows different kinds
     of data to be sent and tagged as different bodyparts.
   - RFC 1494 -- Defines Mapping of single MIME bodypart messages between
     Internet RFC 822 message bodies and X.400 (1988).
   - RFC 1495 -- Defines Mapping of MIME multipart messages between Internet
     and X.400 (1988).
   - RFC 1496 -- Rules for downgrading messages from X.400/88 to X.400/84
     when MIME content-types are present in the messages.
   - RFC 1154 -- Encoding Header Field for Internet Messages. This RFC has
     been superseded by RFC 1521. (RFC 1154 is not supported by MAILbus 400
     SMTP Gateway)

Internet Mail uses a message format defined by RFC 822 for plain text
messages and a transfer protocol called SMTP (Simple Mail Transfer Protocol)
defined by RFC 821. The MIME specification, RFC 1521, extends the definition
of message content to include multipart textual and nontextual information.
X.400 specifies a Message Transfer Service (MTS), which provides a number of
end to end services, including InterPersonal Messaging (IPM). X.400
InterPersonal Messaging provides a framework for user to user communication.

The MIME definitions are used to ensure the highest level of compatibility
for information exchanged between the Internet and X.400. MAILbus 400 SMTP
Gateway is flexible in its operation, providing users with extensive control
over address and data mapping. Users are able to tune the gateway's
operation to match the needs of their particular messaging environment.
Support is also included for 8 bit sendmail systems; the gateway provides
users with control over the interpretation and mapping of information in an
8 bit sendmail environment.

BENEFITS

o  High level of integration with MAILbus 400 and DEC X.500 backbone,
   including accounting and management

o  User addresses are automatically converted between X.400
   originator/recipient (O/R) names and RFC 822 naming conventions and vice
   versa

o  Automatic encoding and decoding of multimedia objects in the MIME format

o  Ongoing support for uuencoding and uudecoding procedures for the exchange
   of mail with non MIME enabled users

o  Generates message delivery reports in line with X.400 standards

o  Support for ISO Latin 1, and 8 bit national characters in the subject
   line

