POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V3.0 for Windows NT

HIGHLIGHTS

o  Industrial-strength enterprise network management capabilities now
   available on Intel based Windows NT platforms

o  A direct port of much of the NetView on Digital UNIX product with
   useability enhancements such as a Windows 95 style graphical user
   interface

o  Integration with Microsoft System Management Server, POLYCENTER
   AssetWORKS, ManageWORKS, and over 40 third party management applications

PRODUCT DESCRIPTION

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V3.0 for Windows NT (SPD 54.68) provides a
comprehensive set of network management tools and a robust framework for
managing enterprisewide networks from Intel based Windows NT platforms.  It
includes a broad range of user-oriented applications for fault,
configuration, and performance management of heterogeneous multivendor
devices and open networks using the Simple Network Management Protocol
(SNMP).  It also includes the industry standard OpenView application
programming interfaces (APIs) that software developers require for
seamlessly integrating their multiprotocol management applications into the
framework.

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT is a direct port of much of the
NetView code base that runs on the Digital UNIX and IBM AIX operating
systems. POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT allows network
managers utilizing Intel Windows NT based management stations to exercise
"industrial-strength"  UNIX like management capabilities over enterprisewide
networks comprised of thousands of nodes.

A highly intuitive graphical user interface with a Windows 95 style

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT combines the power of a full
featured multivendor SNMP manager with the ease of use and familiarity of a
Windows 95 style user interface.  The award-winning NetView graphical user
interface has been further enhanced for the NT environment by giving it the
look-and-feel that predominates the computer industry today.  Users of other
Windows applications will find everything where they expect it to be.
Wizards, menus, toolbars with tooltips, context-sensitive help, and mouse
conventions all work together to ease the task of managing the network.

Extensive useability testing during development by actual network managers
resulted in further specific enhancements to streamline the most common and
frequently-invoked management tasks.

Integration with Microsoft System Management Server and POLYCENTER
AssetWORKS

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT was developed in close
cooperation with Microsoft Corporation to provide integrated management
capabilities with the Microsoft System Management Server.  Through the use
of bidirectional launch capabilities, POLYCENTER NetView for Windows NT can
interoperate with the System Management Server and with POLYCENTER
AssetWorks software to provide a comprehensive and integrated approach to
managing enterprisewide, multivendor hardware and software resources.

POLYCENTER AssetWORKS operates in conjunction with Microsoft System
Management Server to provide a broad range of configuration management
capabilities for multivendor computer systems across the network.  From
POLYCENTER NetView for Windows NT, the network manager can launch Microsoft
System Manager Server and POLYCENTER AssetWorks to display a System
Management Server property sheet for a network or system object. From the
Microsoft System Management Server with POLYCENTER AssetWORKS, the network
or system manager can launch POLYCENTER NetView for Windows NT to find a
network object, diagnose it, show its properties, and graph network traffic.

Integration with ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator

POLYCENTER Manager on NetView for Windows NT can also be used in conjunction
with Digital's ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator product.  ManageWORKS
V2.2, in its role as a PC LAN network and system management solution, is now
able to act as a "mid-level manager," forwarding network status, and SNMP
alarms and events up to an enterprise level manager such as POLYCENTER
NetView for Windows NT. This allows for a combination of centralized
enterprise management from POLYCENTER NetView for Windows NT with
distributed local control provided by ManageWORKS.

Comparison with POLYCENTER Manager on NetView V4.1 for Digital UNIX

This first release of POLYCENTER NetView for Windows NT has been given a
version number of V3.0 to reflect the fact that, while it includes all of
the most critical functions and features that network managers need to
effectively manage enterprise networks, it does not include every feature of
POLYCENTER NetView for Digital UNIX V4.1. The key differences between the
two products are

		      POLYCENTER NetView for  	     POLYCENTER NetView for
                      Digital UNIX V4.1		     Windows NT V3.0

User interface        Motif                   	     Windows 95 style

SMS/AssetWORKS        No                      	     Yes
integration

Database support      Multiple                	     Flat file

Client/server         Yes                     	     In progress

Event correlation     Yes                     	     In progress

Object collection     Yes                     	     In progress

SNMP V2               Yes                     	     In progress

Open topology APIs    Yes                     	     In progress

Control desk          Yes                     	     No

Third party management applications

The enterprise management framework is but one piece of the total network
management solution.  Third party applications provide specific tools that
interoperate with the management platform to provide added-value solutions
in the areas of device management, performance management, security
management, automated operations, LAN management, and help desk services.

For example, StonyBrook Software has been working closely with Digital to
ensure the simultaneous availability of its new RouterManager/AutoBahn
product for Windows NT. This product operates in conjunction with POLYCENTER
NetView for Windows NT to manage routers, bridges, and switches from the
leading device manufacturers such as Cisco, Bay Networks, 3Com, Digital, and
Novell.

To date, over 40 vendors have signed up to support NetView for Windows NT
with their applications and services. For a complete list of these vendors,
refer to the Technical data section of this article.

BENEFITS

o  Full, "UNIX like," industrial strength SNMP management allows network
   managers to experience true enterprise management at NT prices.

o  NetView functions and features ported from the Digital UNIX product make
   POLYCENTER Netview for Windows NT easy to learn and easy to use for
   existing Netview users moving over from the Digital UNIX platform.

o  The Windows 95 style user interface is very familiar to Windows
   applications users, further reducing the learning curve and related
   expenses.

o  Ease-of-use enhancements make managing a network more intuitive, allowing
   users to focus on managing their network rather than on managing Windows.

o  Integration with Microsoft System Management Server and POLYCENTER
   AssetWORKS allows users to achieve full, integrated system and network
   management from one platform.

o  Integration with ManageWORKS Workgroup Administrator allows network
   managers to distribute some network management functions to local
   systems, reducing network traffic load.

o  Integration with over 40 (and growing) third party management
   applications on Windows NT provides users with a complete NT based
