64-bit Challenge Contest

Digital's "64-bit Challenge" is an essay contest that asks contestants to
answer the question, "Why is 64-bit computing important to you?" Contestants
must write an essay explaining the importance of 64-bit computing in their
home or work life. The essay length must be greater than 32 lines, but not
more than 64 lines.

This contest is open to any U.S. resident 18 years or older. Employees of
Digital or any of its affiliates or subsidiaries are not eligible. Each
person may submit only one entry.

All submissions must be received by October 31, 1995. The submissions will
be judged by Digital, and three winners will be selected from the entries
received. Each winner will be notified by electronic mail on November 15,
1995.

The contest prizes are:

o  First place -- an Internet AlphaServer 400 4/166 system
o  Second place -- an AlphaStation 200 4/100 workstation
o  Third place -- a Digital HiNote Ultra laptop PC

All entries must be submitted electronically to contest@kacie.enet.dec.com.
All entries must be in English and include the entrant's full name, the
company name (if appropriate), a valid electronic mail address, and a
telephone number with area code. Certain restrictions apply. This contest is
void where prohibited.

A copy of the contest rules and an entry form can be accessed through the
WWW URL at http://www.digital.com/info/newstuff, or by sending a mail
message to contest@kacie.enet.dec.com.




