(1)The SYSmarkNT benchmarks were generated using similar system
   configurations, consisting of 32 MB of memory, a 1 GB SCSI disk, and a
   Diamond Stealth64 video card. Results are very dependent on the type of
   configuration, with the Matrox 3D card producing slightly lower results.
   Increasing memory in the systems generally increases the scores. Celebris
   XL and Alpha XL scaling is similar for Excel, Texim, and Word products.
   The Celebris XL displays a clear advantage in running emulated 16 bit
   applications (PowerPoint), while the Alpha XL displays a clear advantage
   running more technical applications that use floating point calculations.

(2)ANSYS R5.1 is a solid modeling benchmark program produced by ANSYS, Inc.,
   and supplied to hardware vendors. Each benchmark represents a
   cantilevered plate with one element through its thickness. There are five
   cases, with mesh size varying for the different cases. A force is applied
   to the plate at the free end. CPU time and elapsed time are reported.
   Comparisons are based only on elapsed time, as perceived by the user. All
   tests were performed using 32 MB of memory, 1 GB SCSI disk, and a Matrox
   video card.

(3)The CADalyst benchmark, created by CADalyst Magazine, allows users to
   compare AutoCAD performance across a number of applications: removing
   hidden lines, opening and closing files, regenerating and redrawing the
   screen, creating DXF files, and creating Plotfiles. All tests were
   performed using 32 MB of memory, 1 GB SCSI disk, and a Matrox video card.

(4)Two Pro/Jr. benchmark trail files were used in this test. JRDEMO95 is a
   trail file created by Parametric Technology Corporation that creates
   features, parts, drawings, and assemblies to create a microscope. It also
   includes shaded view manipulations. JRALL is a Digital benchmark file
   that focuses more on feature/part/drawing/assembly manipulations and
   includes operations such as feature reorder, suppress, resume,
   regeneration, and shaded view manipulations, all within part assembly and
   drawing mode. Comparisons are based only on elapsed time, as perceived by
   the user. All tests were performed using 32 MB of memory, 1 GB SCSI disk,
   and a Diamond Stealth video card. As this is a 2D application, the Matrox
   3D card results in 15 to 25 percent less performance for this
   application.

(5)CADbench is a fully automated benchmark program designed to run with
   Microstation software. Developed by Intergraph and Microstation Manager
   magazine, CADbench is a set of tests that focuses on I/O performance,
   vector drawing performance, image rendering performance, and element
   generation performance. The Rendering benchmark consisted of comparisons
   for three different Microstation drawings (oldhotel, locomotive, and
   liv_room). All tests were performed using 32 MB of memory, 1 GB SCSI
   disk, and a Matrox video card. The Celebris systems ran Microstation V5.0
   and the Alpha systems ran Microstation V5.5. Both the Matrox 3D and
   Diamond Stealth 2D performed at similar levels for this application.

